<section class="nc-guide hub-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>5 percentage questions, 1 tool</h2>
    <p>Most everyday percentage problems are one of 5 types, and the <a href="../../calculators/percentage.html">percentage calculator</a> has a mode for each. The trick is recognizing which one you are asking:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>If you're asking…</th><th>It's this type</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>"How much is the 20% discount?"</td><td>X% of Y</td><td>20% of $85 = $17</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"What share of my budget is rent?"</td><td>X is what % of Y</td><td>$1,800 of $6,000 = 30%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"How much did my bill go up?"</td><td>Percent change</td><td>$120 to $138 = +15%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"What's the price with 7% tax?"</td><td>Increase by %</td><td>$50 + 7% = $53.50</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"What's left after 25% off?"</td><td>Decrease by %</td><td>$80 − 25% = $60</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The base is everything</h2>
    <p>A percentage is always a percentage of something, and most mistakes come from using the wrong base. A price that rises from $80 to $100 went up 25%, but a price that falls from $100 to $80 went down 20%, because each change is measured against its own starting point. The same logic means a 50% loss needs a 100% gain to recover, and that "20% off, then another 10% off" is 28% off, not 30%.</p>

    <h2>Quick mental math</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>10%:</strong> move the decimal 1 place left. 10% of $64 is $6.40.</li>
      <li><strong>5%:</strong> half of 10%. 5% of $64 is $3.20.</li>
      <li><strong>15% or 20%:</strong> combine them. 15% of $64 is $6.40 + $3.20 = $9.60; 20% is $12.80.</li>
      <li><strong>Swap the numbers:</strong> X% of Y equals Y% of X. 8% of 50 is the same as 50% of 8, which is 4.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For anything involving money you'll pay or receive, such as discounts, tax, tips, interest, or raises, use the calculator to confirm the mental estimate. Small percentage errors become real dollars on large amounts.</p>
  </div>
</section>
